exFAT filesystem for Linux 2.4 (PlayStation 2)
==============================================

The on-disk format is handled by FatFs R0.16 by ChaN
(https://elm-chan.org/fsw/ff/), with the upstream patches ff16p1 and
ff16p2 (CVE-2026-6682, CVE-2026-6683, CVE-2026-6687) applied.  The Linux
VFS glue is in super.c, namei.c, file.c and misc.c; see exfat_fs.h for
the locking rules.

FatFs files and local changes
-----------------------------

  ff.c, ff.h, ffunicode.c, diskio.h   FatFs R0.16 + patches 1 and 2
  ffconf.h                            configuration for this driver

The sources were converted from CRLF to LF line endings.  Local changes,
all marked "Linux kernel port":

  ff.h    The integer types may be predefined by ffconf.h (FF_INTDEF).
          ff.h only picks them from <stdint.h> on C99 compilers, and
          gcc 2.95 is a C89 compiler.
  ff.c    Uses <linux/string.h> instead of <string.h> under __KERNEL__.
  ff.c    Private __udivdi3() at the end of the file: f_lseek(),
          clmt_clust() and sync_fs() divide 64-bit values and libgcc is
          not linked into the kernel.

When updating FatFs, reapply these three changes, keep FFCONF_DEF in
ffconf.h in step with ff.h, and check with "nm -u exfat.o" that no
libgcc helpers (__udivdi3, __umoddi3, ...) are needed.

FatFs is distributed under a 1-clause BSD license (see the header of
ff.c), which is compatible with the GPL.
